        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blythe%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Blythe’s railroad history began when the California Southern Railroad reached the town in 1916, linking it to Rice, then Blythe Junction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blythe%2C_California",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The city sits in the Palo Verde Valley of the Lower Colorado River Valley, about 224 miles east of Los Angeles and 150 miles west of Phoenix.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blythe%2C_California",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Present‑day Blythe thrives on agriculture and draws winter visitors, tripling its trade area during the season.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
